---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import EvidenceLegend from '../components/EvidenceLegend.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Evidence methodology · Strewnfields"
  description="How Strewnfields extracts atomic claims, preserves coordinate precision, handles conflict, and publishes reviewed meteorite records."
>
  <div class="page-shell">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">Editorial protocol · Version 1</p>
      <h1>From source to field record</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The method is designed to stop plausible-looking map geometry from outrunning its
        evidence.
      </p>
      <EvidenceLegend />
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>1. Preserve the source</h2>
      <p>
        We record bibliographic metadata, URL or DOI, access date, and a page, figure,
        field, table, or message locator. A bibliography alone is not enough to trace an
        atomic claim.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Extract claims, not conclusions</h2>
      <p>
        Identity, classification, location, date, mass, recovery narrative, and extent
        become separate assertions. Contradictions remain separate. We do not average
        conflicting locations or select a preferred number solely because it is newer.
      </p>

      <h2 id="coordinate-precision">3. Preserve coordinate meaning</h2>
      <p>
        Original notation and stated datum or coordinate reference system are retained.
        WGS84 decimal values are arithmetic normalizations for software, not newly measured
        positions. Precision and estimated accuracy are distinct:
      </p>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Precision</h3>
          <p>How finely a source writes a value, such as a whole arc-minute.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Accuracy</h3>
          <p>
            How closely the value is expected to match the real-world thing it claims to
            locate.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Semantic role</h3>
          <p>
            What the coordinate means: locality, individual recovery, search area, or
            derived model.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>
        A value can be numerically precise yet geographically unsuitable. The interface
        keeps source notation and semantic roles beside normalized map positions, and
        records with no approved public points remain map-free.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Assess evidence and confidence separately</h2>
      <p>
        Evidence labels describe provenance and role. Confidence records editorial
        assessment of a particular assertion. “Official” is reserved for identified
        nomenclature status or authority; it is never shorthand for exact, peer reviewed, or
        correct.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Keep derivation inspectable</h2>
      <p>
        Any project-created geometry must cite every input and state its method, coordinate
        system, precision, accuracy limits, and sensitivity. The catalogue does not draw a
        strewnfield polygon when public evidence does not support an anchored orientation
        and perimeter.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Review before publication</h2>
      <p>
        Automated validation checks schema shape, source references, coordinate roles, and
        public safety fields. Language-model review can flag inconsistencies but is not a
        source. Human editorial approval is required for publication.
      </p>

      <h2 id="location-safety">Location and safety policy</h2>
      <p>
        Legally reusable public coordinates may still be withheld or generalized for active
        research, embargoes, private residences, protected or culturally sensitive land,
        ecological risk, or a credible safety request. Membership never overrides a
        restriction. Coordinates never imply ownership, permission to enter, permission to
        collect, or legality of possession or export.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Corrections should identify the claim, cite a reviewable source, and explain the
        proposed change. Do not place private coordinates or personal information in a
        public issue. Urgent suppression concerns can be raised without publishing the
        sensitive value itself.
      </p>
      <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/rayborg/strewnfields/issues"
        >Open an issue</a
      >
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
